Compile-time facts about the build, as function-like macros: the compiler, the target
operating system and CPU, whether a builtin exists, and whether debug checks are on. They are
the only place ca tests predefined macros (`__clang__`, `__linux__`, `__x86_64__`, ...), so
porting means editing this folder.

| Macro | Value |
|---|---|
| `ca_compiler(clang)`, `ca_compiler(gcc)` | 1 for the compiler building the code, else 0; any other compiler fails to compile |
| `ca_platform(linux)`, `ca_platform(macos)` | 1 for the target OS; other systems fail to compile |
| `ca_architecture(x86_64)`, `(aarch64)`, `(riscv64)` | 1 for the target CPU; other CPUs build, all 0 |
| `ca_target_feature(sse2)` | 1 when the build may use those instructions; 0 on another CPU and in an integer-only build (`-mgeneral-regs-only`, `-mno-sse`) |
| `ca_target_feature(floats)` | 1 when the build may use floating-point registers; 0 only in an integer-only build (x86-64 without SSE2, aarch64 `-mgeneral-regs-only`, riscv64 without F), 1 on another CPU |
| `ca_has_builtin(name)` | `__has_builtin(name)`, or 0 where the compiler lacks it |
| `ca_debug` | 1 unless `NDEBUG` is defined; may be predefined to 0 or 1 |

The macros expand to expressions, so they work in `#if` and in ordinary code alike. A
misspelled name is a compile error rather than a silent 0, since each expands to a function
macro named after its argument.

```c++
#include "ca/config.hpp"

#if ca_compiler(clang)
inline constexpr const char compiler_name[] = "clang";
#else
inline constexpr const char compiler_name[] = "gcc";
#endif

#if ca_has_builtin(__builtin_expect)
#	define likely_true(x) __builtin_expect(!!(x), 1)
#else
#	define likely_true(x) (x)
#endif

auto main(void) -> int {
	if constexpr (ca_architecture(x86_64))
		return compiler_name[0] == '\0';
	return likely_true(ca_platform(linux) || ca_platform(macos)) ? 0 : 1;
}
```

`ca_debug` turns on the checked-access rule of the uncertain containers (`optional`,
`result`: reading the value before testing it fails, at compile time when the optimizer can
prove it) and the block checks of `ca::heap` on deallocation. Building with `-DNDEBUG` turns
them off; `-Dca_debug=1` keeps them in a release build. Accessing an absent value traps in
every build, whatever `ca_debug` says.
